0.1.16 (2026-11-18)
  * ORDER 25: Phone as a camera, a package entry of Graphics, Others (`kind: apt`, `sizes: true`), beside Kamoso and Webcamoid in Pawel's page of both trees. It installs sparky-stereo-phone-camera, which depends on DroidCam's client (droidcam), scrcpy (its camera mode, the fully open way), adb and v4l2loopback-dkms; the app center depends on none of them, so the upgrade from 0.1.15 installs one package.
  * The card says in the ten languages what each program is and what the phone needs (the DroidCam app from the phone's own store; USB debugging and Android 12 or newer for scrcpy), that scrcpy's package fetches its small phone-side server from the project's release page and checks its SHA-256, and that the phone appears as the camera "Phone camera". New test: stereo/tests/phonecamera.py (8 cases).
  * The source pointer is the app center fork's order25 (b4b9dec) on e1f7d82 (0.1.15's pointer).

0.1.15 (2026-11-18)
  * ORDER 10: the store-link kind (`kind: store`), for a vendor who allows exactly a link: CODESYS 4 is its first row. Before, CODESYS 4 was a card hard-coded in the page whose address was opened by Pawel's handler with x-www-browser, as the administrator the app center runs as, and whose only sentence was "Registration and the EULA are managed by CODESYS in its store." Now the card says before the button, in the ten languages, what CODESYS 4 is (a web-based IEC 61131-3 development environment, listed by CODESYS for Windows x64, Linux x64 and Linux ARM64, used in a web browser), that CODESYS GmbH makes it and owns its names, that the button opens the CODESYS Store in the web browser, that the person registers there if it asks and downloads under CODESYS's own licence agreement, that Stereo APTus installs and downloads nothing, and the address as text.
  * The click runs xdg-open as the person: run as administrator (the app center starts through pkexec) it drops to the user pkexec names with setpriv and never opens a browser as root; a refused address, a missing xdg-open, no known person or an xdg-open failure is said in a window with the address to open by hand. The address (https, the vendor's own domain) is data in the row, checked at the click by storelink.py and in the data by stereo/tests/store.py (24 cases). The page makes no request of its own.
  * New file: /usr/lib/sparky-aptus-stereo-appcenter/storelink.py. The card stays where the old one was, last in "Stereo players, editors and tools". Every other entry is byte for byte the same; the ten required language files gain ten keys each and the older keys stay.
  * Depends are the same as 0.1.14: nothing is installed or fetched for a link.
  * The source pointer is the app center fork's order10 (e1f7d82), on master bc704ac (0.1.14's pointer).

0.1.14 (2026-11-18)
  * ORDER 17 (milestone 7): the Stereo Model Viewer (sparky-stereo-model-viewer) is an entry of "Stereo players, editors and tools", on the same page as F3D and CloudCompare: a small Kirigami window on libf3d with a declared stereo area. A test case pins it (section, kind, package, visibility, the exact apt command).
  * The source pointer is the app center fork's order17i (bc704ac): master 477869c (0.1.13, with ORDER 18's PJeOffice Pro entry, unchanged) plus this entry. Depends are the same as 0.1.13.

0.1.13 (2026-11-18)
  * ORDER 18 (milestone 2): PJeOffice Pro, CNJ's signer for the court e-filing system, is an entry of Office, Documents (Paweł's page carries the link, in both trees). It installs sparky-pjeoffice, which fetches CNJ's program from CNJ's own address when it is set up: the card says before the button the size and the address of that download, apt's sizes for the package and what it depends on and recommends (OpenSC, pcscd, libccid), and the metered notice when the setting asks for it; a failed setup shows the engine's sentence. Nothing of CNJ's is in this package.
  * The first package entry of a main page: `actions/entry status ID` answers with the download and the sizes, `kept_problem` adds the package's own reason to apt's exit status, `mainpages.render_apt` draws the card. The sentence of the card is in the ten languages. Tests: stereo/tests/pjeoffice.py (15 cases).
  * Depends are the same as 0.1.12: nothing the program needs is a dependency of this package. The same entry for SparkyLinux's main app center is the fork's branch order18b-sparkylinux (Paweł's own scripts), prepared and not sent.

0.1.12 (2026-11-18)
  * ORDER 17 (milestone 5): CloudCompare is an entry of "Stereo players, editors and tools": the point-cloud and mesh program the edition ships in stereo (cloudcompare 2.13.2+git20240821+ds-7+stereo3d1+lc1: its Enable 3D draws the 3D area as a declared pair inside the window, with the separation from the person's depth limits). An apt entry like F3D and Blender beside it; it is offered when the sources offer the package and installs at the candidate, the edition's build when the edition's repository is configured.
  * A test case pins it (section, kind, package, visibility, the exact apt command).
  * Depends are the same as 0.1.11. Paweł's main pages carry no CloudCompare entry (read in the copied 9/ tree).

0.1.11 (2026-11-16)
  * ORDER 589: the Vinci entry offers Vinci 0.4.9 (14 November 2026, by Dionatan Simioni), pinned by its release address, its size (38,386,168 bytes) and its SHA-256, in the entry and in main's own scripts that this package carries (sparky-aptus-appcenter pull request #3 of the edition's fork, a8c1e77). 0.1.10 pinned 0.4.7.
  * The package's source tests run again: seven cases failed on the station and before the Vinci change too, none of them a fault of the program. The tests find the install-time download reader and Paweł's pages themselves and say which one is missing, and two expectations that ORDER 553 (0.1.7 and 0.1.8: the appimage kind, the main section) and ORDER 565 and 575 (sentences in the ten required languages) had outdated now follow the accepted behaviour.
  * Depends are the same as 0.1.10: nothing Vinci needs is a dependency of this package.

0.1.10 (2026-11-15)
  * ORDER 575 (Daniel, 2026-11-14, on the Info Centre line "DisplayCAL is not installed": "keep it off by default, it requires the hardware"; then "include a require calibrator hardware"): DisplayCAL is an entry of Tools and utilities, offered and never installed by default. Its card says that DisplayCAL requires calibrator hardware (a colorimeter or a spectrophotometer) and, before the button, apt's sizes for Debian's displaycal and what it depends on (the one tools mechanism of 0.1.9: the row says "sizes", so it simulates the entry's own program as the action installs it).
  * `actions/entry status ID` answers for one entry (installed, offered by the sources, sizes or the reason it cannot be installed) without the whole page's cost; the Info Centre asks it.
  * An install that fails says what failed and what to do, in the ten languages: apt busy, no network, a package no source offers, or apt's exit status.
  * Depends are the same as 0.1.9: the app center never depends on DisplayCAL or ArgyllCMS; they arrive when the person installs the entry.

0.1.9 (2026-11-14)
  * ORDER 565 (Daniel, 2026-11-14: "only when someone install vinci - same for anything that needs this sort of dependancy"): the tools an entry's program needs arrive when the person installs that entry, never as dependencies of this package. An entry declares them once (`tools`); the entry's action reads apt's own simulation, says which tools, how many packages and what they cost to download and on disk before anything starts, joins them to the metered notice of sparky-stereo-apt's reader, downloads every archive first and then installs. Tools already on the system are left as they are: no reinstall, no upgrade, no notice.
  * Depends no longer names podman, distrobox, fuse3, bsdextrautils (Vinci, KamLynk) or flatpak (SlimeVR): an upgrade from 0.1.6 installs none of them. Vinci declares podman, distrobox, fuse3 and bsdextrautils; KamLynk declares fuse3; a Flatpak entry needs flatpak. 0.1.8, which depended on the four, was never published.
  * Every failure is said and stops the entry: a tool apt cannot install (with apt's reason, before any download), archives that cannot be downloaded (nothing installed), a failed installation, and a program download that fails. In the ten languages.
  * When an entry is removed, the tools it brought stay installed and are marked as automatically installed unless another installed entry needs them, so apt's own rule decides when they go. A tool that was there before is not touched, except one apt had installed automatically: it is marked manual while the entry is installed and automatic again afterwards. An installation that fails after its tools arrived gives them back to apt the same way.
  * Everything else is 0.1.8: main's pages, Vinci on Multimedia, Video tools, KamLynk on Accessories, Devices, the stereo sections and the catalogue as published.

